# Clean Code Guidelines

## Scope / Flexibility Notice
These guidelines promote writing clean, maintainable, and readable code throughout the project. While domain-specific implementations may vary, these core principles should be consistently applied.

## Best Practices
- Follow the Single Responsibility Principle - each class and method should do one thing well
- Keep functions and methods small and focused (ideally < 30 lines)
- Use intention-revealing names for variables, functions, and classes
- Avoid deep nesting of code blocks (limit to 2-3 levels when possible)
- Minimize the use of comments - prefer self-documenting code
- Don't repeat yourself (DRY) - extract repetitive code into reusable functions
- Avoid long parameter lists (>3 parameters) - use parameter objects when needed
- Fail fast - validate inputs early in functions
- Avoid global state and side effects
- Write code for humans first, machines second
- Use early returns to reduce nesting and complexity
- Extract complex conditional logic into named functions or variables
- Follow verb phrases for functions (e.g., `fetchUser()`, `calculateTotal()`)
- Prefix booleans with verbs (e.g., `isEnabled`, `hasPermission`)
- Break down complex classes into smaller, focused ones
- Use named parameters for clarity and self-documentation

## File / Project Structure Guidelines
- Organize related code into cohesive files and classes
- Keep files reasonably sized (usually < 300-400 lines)
- Group related functions and methods together
- Organize imports in a consistent order:
  ```dart
  // Dart imports
  import 'dart:async';
  
  // Package imports
  import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
  
  // Project imports (relative)
  import '../models/user.dart';
  ```
- Separate interface from implementation when appropriate
- One class per file as a general rule (with exceptions for tightly coupled classes)
- Group code by feature rather than by type
- Create dedicated directories for reusable components
- Structure feature directories consistently:
  ```
  lib/
    features/
      auth/
        domain/
          models/
          repositories/
        presentation/
          screens/
          widgets/
        application/
          cubits/
  ```

## Coding & Styling Conventions
- Use consistent and descriptive naming:
  - Variables/parameters: `lowerCamelCase`
  - Classes/Types: `UpperCamelCase`
  - Constants: `kConstantName` or `SCREAMING_CAPS` for truly fixed values
- Avoid abbreviations and acronyms unless universally understood
- Prefix boolean variables with verbs like `is`, `has`, `should`, etc.
- Keep line length reasonable (ideally < 80 characters)
- Use trailing commas for improved formatting in multi-line structures
- Use dartfmt/flutter format for consistent styling
- Prefer named parameters for better readability in complex method calls
- Make fields private unless they need external access
- Use getters and setters for controlled property access
- Group related properties and methods within classes
- Implement proper error handling patterns:
  - Use typed exceptions
  - Avoid swallowing exceptions silently
  - Handle errors at appropriate levels

## Code Comments and Documentation
- Write self-documenting code with clear names and structure
- Add comments only when necessary to explain "why" not "what"
- Document public APIs with dartdoc comments
- Use code comments for:
  - Explaining complex algorithms
  - Documenting non-obvious decisions
  - Flagging potential issues or edge cases
  - Explaining temporary workarounds
- Examples:
  ```dart
  /// Authenticates a user with email and password.
  ///
  /// Returns a [User] object if authentication is successful.
  /// Throws [AuthException] if authentication fails.
  /// 
  /// Parameters:
  /// - [email]: The user's email address
  /// - [password]: The user's password (min 8 characters)
  Future<User> authenticate(String email, String password) async {
    // Implementation
  }
  
  // GOOD: Comment explains WHY, not just WHAT
  double calculateDiscount(double total, String userType) {
    // Premium users get 20% off their entire order,
    // while regular users get 10% off only when total exceeds $100
    if (userType == 'premium') {
      return total * 0.2;
    } else if (total > 100) {
      return total * 0.1;
    }
    return 0;
  }
  ```

## Performance & Optimization Tips
- Avoid premature optimization - focus on clean, correct code first
- Identify bottlenecks through profiling before optimizing
- Consider readability impacts when making performance optimizations
- Document performance-sensitive code sections with benchmark results
- Avoid expensive computations in build() methods or constructors
- Prefer const constructors when creating immutable widgets
- Extract complex widget methods to improve readability:
  ```dart
  // BAD: Deeply nested conditional logic
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      body: isLoading 
          ? Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator())
          : hasError
              ? Center(child: Text('An error occurred'))
              : data.isEmpty
                  ? Center(child: Text('No items found'))
                  : ListView.builder(
                      itemCount: data.length,
                      itemBuilder: (context, index) {
                        return ListTile(title: Text(data[index].name));
                      },
                    ),
    );
  }
  
  // GOOD: Extracted widget methods for clarity
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      body: _buildBody(context),
    );
  }
  
  Widget _buildBody(BuildContext context) {
    if (isLoading) {
      return _buildLoadingIndicator();
    }
    
    if (hasError) {
      return _buildErrorMessage();
    }
    
    if (data.isEmpty) {
      return _buildEmptyState();
    }
    
    return _buildDataList();
  }
  ```

## Testing & Validation Guidelines
- Write tests for complex logic and edge cases
- Test behavior, not implementation details
- Structure tests with clear Arrange-Act-Assert sections
- Use descriptive test names that explain the behavior being tested
- Write tests that fail when the code is broken
- Keep tests independent of each other
- Use mocks judiciously for dependencies outside your control
- Write testable code:
  - Use dependency injection
  - Avoid global state
  - Separate business logic from UI
- Test edge cases and error conditions
- Maintain high test coverage for critical paths
